{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p7-e5bf5587","text":"Interesting: tables exist (the error is "permission denied for table weddings" not "relation does not exist"), so schema was applied but no GRANTs were issued to anon/authenticated. That's a real bug in schema.sql: creating tables in the SQL editor usually runs as `postgres` which does not automatically grant privileges in some cases... Actually Supabase's SQL editor runs as `postgres` role; tables created by postgres in public schema do get default privileges? Supabase sets default privileges granting to anon/authenticated via `alter default privileges`? In newer Supabase projects, `postgres` default privileges do include grants... apparently not here. Hmm — the error hints grants are missing.

Wait: 401 with code 42501 permission denied — yes, missing GRANTs. But also note: service role bypasses, so 200 for auth admin works.

So the schema is deployed but RLS grants are missing → even the anon client can't read. The dashboard as authenticated user would also fail with permission denied. This is the bug blocking "customer can create a QR code".

