## What actually happened in the app

Logging in **works**: the browser got a valid session from `mzxgxnswopplwsbwdiyr.supabase.co` and was redirected to `/admin`. Then the dashboard's five reads (`weddings`, `guests`, `media`, `likes`, `messages`) all came back **403 `permission denied for table weddings` (Postgres 42501)**. The same error hits the `service_role` key too, so it isn't RLS — the tables exist but were never granted to the API roles (`anon`, `authenticated`, `service_role`). Creating a wedding fails the same way, and the QR code needs a saved wedding row, so the flow stops before any QR can exist.

One side effect worth knowing: the dashboard swallows that failure and sits on "Opening your events…" forever instead of showing an error, so a customer would just see a spinner. I left that alone since you asked me to test, not change it.

## What I changed

- [schema.sql](supabase/schema.sql) — added a Privileges section (`grant … to anon, authenticated, service_role` plus default privileges) so a fresh install can't hit this again.
- [fix-grants.sql](supabase/fix-grants.sql) — new, idempotent one-paste fix for the database you already have.

No application code was touched. I tried to apply the SQL myself, but the Supabase tab in the preview panel turned out to be a sign-in screen rather than an authenticated SQL editor, and the CLI on this machine is logged into a different account that can't see this project.

