Added a 20-second cap that sits next to your existing size limits and blocks the selection before any bytes are uploaded.

## What changed

**[src/lib/cloudinary.js](src/lib/cloudinary.js)** — new `MAX_VIDEO_SECONDS = 20` plus `getVideoDuration(file)`, which loads the clip into a throwaway `<video>` element from a blob URL and reads its metadata locally. No network, no Cloudinary call. It handles the `Infinity` duration that fragmented MP4 and MediaRecorder WebM report by seeking to a huge timestamp once to force the browser to scan the end, and it bails out after 5 s with `null` if the file's duration can't be determined at all.

**[src/components/wedding/UploadModal.jsx](src/components/wedding/UploadModal.jsx)** — `validate()` is now async: for each picked video it checks size first, then duration, and rejects with a named message. While it's measuring, the Share button reads "Checking length…" and is disabled, so nobody can submit a clip mid-check. The picker hint now reads "Videos up to 100 MB and 20 seconds."

Threshold is 20.5 s rather than exactly 20, so a clip that genuinely runs 20.0–20.4 s isn't rejected over a rounding artefact. Rejections look like:

> "sample-30s.mp4" is 30s long — videos can be up to 20 seconds.

**[README.md](README.md)** — the free-tier notes row now documents the duration gate.

## Verified in the browser

I ran the exact probe the modal uses, plus the real `<input type=file>` path on the guest gallery:

| Clip | Reported | Probe time | Outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| flower.mp4 (5.05 s, 1.1 MB) | 5.055 s | 50 ms | accepted, Share enabled, listed as `flower.mp4` |
| sample-30s.mp4 (30.44 s, 21 MB) | 30.441 s | 16 ms | rejected with the message above, Share stayed disabled |

`npm run build` passes (exit 0). Temporary test videos were deleted; `git status` shows only the three intended files modified.

## One limitation worth knowing

This is a **client-side gate**, the same class as the 20 MB / 100 MB limits — it stops honest guests, not a determined one, since the upload goes browser → Cloudinary with an unsigned preset. If you want a hard guarantee, add an incoming transformation of `du_20` to the preset in Cloudinary (Settings → Upload → your preset → Upload manipulation), which trims anything longer server-side. Also: when a browser can't decode a clip's metadata (e.g. iPhone HEVC `.mov` on Android Chrome), the probe returns nothing after 5 s and the file is **allowed** rather than blocked — a deliberate choice so odd-but-valid formats aren't rejected outright. Restricting the preset's allowed formats to `jpg,png,heic,mp4,mov` keeps that hole small.

Like the previous fix, this needs a redeploy before it applies to your live Netlify site.
