{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p4-9336cf7b","text":"The badge is injected at the edge, not in our source. Official way to remove: Project configuration → General → Powered by Netlify badge → off → Save. Takes effect next request; no redeploy. Free plan allows turning it off (docs explicitly say you can turn it off, e.g. for client work).\n\nSo no code change. I should also mention CSP alternative is not recommended (it's a workaround, and could break other things if CSP added wrongly).\n\nI can't toggle it from here (no Netlify access/credentials). Answer with steps. No files to change → answer only. Maybe also mention if they later move to a custom domain the badge still follows the project setting.\n\nAlso note: badge appears only on public projects; new free projects from Aug 19 2026 default on.\n\nShould I check whether their site actually shows it via preview? Their deployed URL might be in netlify? Unknown URL. Not needed.\n\nAnswer concisely, then suggest prompts.","open":false,"collapse":"preview"}
That badge isn't in your code — it's injected by Netlify's edge servers, so there's nothing in the repo to delete. Search the project and you'll find `netlify` only in [netlify.toml](netlify.toml) comments.

According to Netlify's own documentation, it's a per-project setting you can simply switch off, and free-plan projects created on or after 19 August 2026 default to on — which is why yours has it. [Netlify docs: Powered by Netlify badge](https://docs.netlify.com/manage/projects/powered-by-netlify-badge/)

## Turn it off

1. Open your project in the Netlify UI.
2. **Project configuration → General → Powered by Netlify badge**.
3. Toggle it off and **Save**.

The change applies on the next request — no redeploy, no env var, no config file. Netlify explicitly allows turning it off when third-party branding doesn't suit the project (client work being their example), so this isn't a workaround or a terms violation.

I can't do this step for you: it lives in your Netlify account settings, and I have no access to it. It's a 20-second click.

## Alternatives, and one I'd avoid

- **A visitor-level hide** exists — clicking the badge opens a card with "Hide this badge" — but it's stored in that browser's local storage for that domain only, so other guests still see it. Not what you want.
- **Blocking it with a Content Security Policy** works, since the badge script runs in an inline frame and a `script-src` without `'unsafe-inline'` stops it. But adding a CSP just to hide a badge is a bad trade: a careless policy also breaks Supabase, Cloudinary uploads, Google Fonts, or the badge-less page itself. Don't take this route.
- **Moving to Cloudflare Pages** would remove it by default, but that's a hosting migration to solve a toggle.

One thing worth knowing: the badge is per project, not per domain, so when you attach your custom wedding domain the setting you just saved still applies. Nothing else in the app changes, and the toggle has no effect on your build, your QR codes, or uploads.
